// HELENA BLAVATSKY: THE STANZAS OF DZYAN (THE SECRET DOCTRINE, 1888) AND
// SELECTED PASSAGES FROM ISIS UNVEILED (1877) AND THE SECRET DOCTRINE.
//
// The text comes from blavatsky-text.ts, generated by
// scripts/texts/blavatsky-dzyan.py from the Theosophical University Press
// online edition (which keeps the first editions' pages), compared word by word
// with the archive.org OCRs of three or four separate printed copies of each
// volume, with the readings the OCRs could not settle checked on the page
// images. Every passage is cited by the volume and page of the first edition.
//
// A selection, not the whole books. Its units (divisionKind "section") are:
//   - the Stanzas of Cosmogenesis, I–VII (SD I, pp. 27–34), one unit per stanza;
//   - the Stanzas of Anthropogenesis, I–XII, slokas 1–49 (SD II, pp. 15–21),
//     one unit per stanza;
//   - twenty passages of Blavatsky's own prose on the book she claims to quote,
//     Hermes and Egypt, the Smaragdine (Emerald) Tablet, Atlantis and the root
//     races, nine from Isis Unveiled and eleven from The Secret Doctrine.
// The Stanzas are kept apart from her commentary on them: they are imported as
// she first printed them together, before the commentary that repeats them
// sloka by sloka. They are grouped under five "part" records: the Isis
// selection, the SD vol. I selection, the two sets of Stanzas, and the SD vol.
// II selection.
//
// THE BOOK OF DZYAN. Blavatsky presents the Stanzas as translated from an
// archaic "Book of Dzyan" in a secret language, Senzar. No manuscript of such a
// book has ever been produced, no scholar has seen one, and she herself wrote
// that it was "utterly unknown to our Philologists" (SD I, p. xxii). W. E.
// Coleman (1895) called it "the work of Madame Blavatsky, — a compilation, in
// her own language, from a variety of sources". The archive therefore records
// its antiquity only as her claim, and dates the Stanzas' composition to the
// 1880s, before their first printing in 1888. Her links to ancient texts are
// recorded as what they are: she quotes, cites or retells them; none of the
// links claims ancient transmission to her or from her.
//
// Nothing is marked verified, and the selection is not a stated inventory of
// either book (inventoryVerified stays false).

